 "figures": {
  "roundthree": {
   "title": "What round 3 says about a local repair and a local share-out",
   "caption": "Each dot is one of the 70 worlds of Experiment 40 that lived to the end of their 3,000 iterations, read from iteration 100 on; the short bar is the median of a column. Top left, on a logarithmic axis: the share of all the world's tokens a game culls, the tokens of agents cut off from the largest piece of the network, for the worlds drawn with mending (a dying agent's former neighbours joined up again in a ring) and those without. Top right: the most of its agents a single game culled. Bottom left: how wide the world is, its typical distance over that of a random network with the same links (on the dashed line, no wider than one). Bottom right, on a logarithmic axis: what the richest agent holds, by where each world's rule sends the tokens of the dead: evenly over all, to the poorest, to this iteration's newborns, to their parents, to the dead agent's own children, or to the nearest living. Every other setting of each world was drawn at random too, so each column mixes many kinds of world.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** the worlds of round 3 of the screen of random worlds that lived to the end of their 3,000 iterations (Experiment 40: 30,000 tokens, every setting drawn at random)\n\n**Data.** From each world's averages over iterations 100 to the end, `research/screen/health.json` (from the screen's readings, `research/screen/measures.py`), and each run's own statistics, `GraphOfLifeRuns/<run>/stats.jsonl` (`nodes_before`, `starved`, `orphaned`).\n\n1. Read each living world's averages from `health.json`: `culled_tokens` (the share of all tokens a game's cleanup shares out, every one a culled agent's), `width` and `richest`.\n2. From its `stats.jsonl`, for every game from iteration 100 on, `orphaned` over `nodes_before`: the share of the agents the game culled. Keep the largest.\n3. Group the worlds by their drawn `mend_on_death`, and by `redistribution`.\n4. One dot per world; the bar is the median.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py localrules`."
  }
